        <h1 class="paper-title">Compile-Time Lookup Tables for Inference-Latency-Bounded Lock Selection in the Linux Kernel: A Constraint Review and Feasibility Study</h1>
        <div class="author-block">
            <div class="author-col">
                <div class="author-name">Chandru G</div>
                <div class="author-dept">Department of Computer Science and Engineering (AI &amp; ML)</div>
                <div class="author-dept">PES University, Bengaluru, Karnataka, India</div>
            </div>
            <div class="author-col">
                <div class="author-name">Chethan S</div>
                <div class="author-dept">Department of Computer Science and Engineering (AI &amp; ML)</div>
                <div class="author-dept">PES University, Bengaluru, Karnataka, India</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cols">
        <p class="no-indent"><strong><em>Abstract</em>&mdash;Multicore Linux systems experience severe scalability bottlenecks under lock contention on NUMA servers. While machine learning (ML) can classify contention to select optimal synchronization primitives, hot-path deployment encounters a physical latency wall: lock acquisitions require decisions within 10&ndash;100&nbsp;ns, whereas in-kernel tree traversal incurs branch mispredictions and memory overheads. We present CLTML, compiling an offline-trained gradient-boosted decision tree (GBDT) into a 4&nbsp;KB L1-resident lookup table evaluated via branchless fixed-point indexing with zero floating-point operations. In microbenchmarks on an [[HOST_CPU_CLEAN]] running CachyOS Linux 7.3, CLTML evaluates in [[LAT_4X3]]&nbsp;ns ([[CYC_4X3]] invariant TSC cycles)&mdash;a 24.1&times; speedup over branchless integer GBDTs ([[LAT_INT_GBDT]]&nbsp;ns) and 3.3&times; over a depth-3 decision tree (12.40&nbsp;ns)&mdash;while retaining [[ACC_4X3]]% selection accuracy ([[LOSS_4X3]]&nbsp;pp vs. unconstrained float ensemble) and bounding mean regret to [[REGRET_4X3]]&nbsp;ns. End-to-end multi-threaded benchmarks demonstrate +15% to +49% net throughput gains across 20 workload regimes. We validate four userspace locks across asymmetric cores, analytically project 64-core NUMA scaling, and demonstrate kernel deployability via an out-of-tree module implementing Tier&nbsp;1 spin-ceiling tuning with zero sleep-in-atomic violations; Tier&nbsp;2 primitive switching is formalized as an architectural design.</strong></p>
        <p class="no-indent" style="margin-top: 3px;"><strong><em>Index Terms</em>—Lock contention, Linux kernel synchronization, NUMA scalability, gradient-boosted decision trees, lookup-table compilation, inference latency, learned lock selection, in-kernel machine learning.</strong></p>

        <h2 class="sec-heading">I. Introduction and Research Questions</h2>
        <h3 class="subsec-heading">A. Problem Motivation</h3>
        <p>Multicore Linux servers experience a severe scalability cliff when thread counts scale across multiple Non-Uniform Memory Access (NUMA) sockets. In comprehensive empirical evaluations across 28 synchronization algorithms on 48-core four-socket systems, David et al. [1] demonstrated that lock waiting times scale super-linearly once thread contention crosses socket boundaries. On modern x86_64 servers, an L1 data cache hit requires 1.0 to 1.5&nbsp;ns (4–5 cycles) and a local L3 cache hit requires 10 to 15&nbsp;ns (30–45 cycles), whereas remote cache-line transfers across inter-socket interconnects (UPI/Infinity Fabric) incur 100 to 200&nbsp;ns (300–600 cycles)—an order-of-magnitude latency disparity. Similarly, Guiroux et al. [2] evaluated 19 lock algorithms across 36 multi-threaded applications and proved that no single static lock algorithm achieves optimal throughput and tail latency across all workloads.</p>

        <p>Every static kernel primitive trades one failure mode for another. Linux's queued spinlock (<code>qspinlock</code>) [6] bounds cache-line coherence traffic to O(1) via per-CPU Mellor-Crummey and Scott (MCS) queue nodes when three or more threads contend, but incurs pointer setup overhead and hands off locks in strict FIFO order, lacking NUMA socket awareness unless paired with Compact NUMA-Aware (CNA) extensions [8]. Hierarchical NUMA cohort locks [5] achieve 2.1× to 3.2× throughput gains on multi-socket servers by passing locks locally within a socket, but expose remote waiters to starvation exceeding 500&nbsp;µs under sustained local load. Read-Copy-Update (RCU) [3, 7] eliminates reader lock overhead entirely, yet incurs grace-period synchronization latency and memory pressure when write ratios rise above 15% to 20%.</p>

        <p>Machine learning (ML) offers an appealing paradigm to observe hardware performance counter (PMC) signals—such as Last-Level Cache (LLC) miss rates, cross-NUMA interconnect traffic, lock queue depth, and read/write ratios—and select the lowest-cost synchronization policy dynamically. Profiling studies by Boyd-Wickizer et al. [16] demonstrate that fewer than 8% of kernel lock acquisition sites account for over 85% of total lock wait time, indicating that dynamic adaptation at bottleneck sites can yield system-wide scalability gains. Furthermore, Lozi et al. [19] documented that synchronization bottlenecks in the Linux scheduler cascade into core starvation and severe multicore underutilization. However, deploying ML inside the kernel lock acquisition hot path encounters a physical latency wall: the uncontended compare-and-swap (CAS) fast path executes in 15 to 25&nbsp;ns, and the contended queue handoff budget is bounded by 50 to 100&nbsp;ns, whereas runtime evaluation of gradient-boosted decision trees (GBDTs) or neural networks requires 250 to 1,200&nbsp;ns. Crucially, uncontended lock acquisitions (where compare-and-swap succeeds immediately in 15&ndash;25&nbsp;ns) execute with zero CLTML evaluation; CLTML operates strictly upon contention within the optimistic spin loop or slow path, where decision latencies under 10&nbsp;ns yield 25&ndash;150&nbsp;ns synchronization savings.</p>

        <h3 class="subsec-heading">B. Research Questions and Contributions</h3>
        <p class="no-indent">This study investigates three explicit, quantitatively bounded Research Questions (RQs):</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>RQ1 (Deployment Constraints &amp; Synchronization Trade-Offs):</strong> What architectural, latency, and context-safety constraints govern Linux kernel synchronization, and how do static, heuristic, and learned approaches satisfy or violate them across NUMA regimes?</li>
            <li><strong>RQ2 (Latency Budget &amp; Fast-Path Feasibility):</strong> What decision latencies do existing in-kernel ML and learned synchronization systems exhibit, and why does runtime tree inference violate the sub-100&nbsp;ns fast-path ceiling?</li>
            <li><strong>RQ3 (Quantifiable Conditions for Feasible LUT Compilation):</strong> Under what concrete bounds (feature count &le; 4, quantization bitwidth &le; 12 bits, L1 cache residency &le; 4&nbsp;KB) can an offline-trained GBDT be compiled into a branchless lookup table achieving &lt;4&nbsp;ns inline latency without accuracy collapse?</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Amortization vs. Per-Acquisition Responsiveness:</strong> A frequent objection is whether lock selection can simply be amortized once per epoch (e.g., every 1,024 acquisitions) using a background thread. While per-epoch evaluation amortizes computational overhead to &lt;1&nbsp;ns, it suffers from catastrophic reaction lag: in realistic multi-threaded workloads, bursty contention phase shifts occur rapidly across 45 to 120 lock acquisitions&mdash;derived from our workload-shift simulation calibrated to PARSEC and SPECjbb transactional distributions. An amortized interval of 1,024 acquisitions lags behind these bursty micro-phases by an entire order of magnitude. Holding a decision constant across 1,024 acquisitions during phase transitions causes selection accuracy to collapse from 95.23% to 21.95% (below static qspinlock's 35.27%, Table IV), inflating regret to 136.39&nbsp;ns. Sensitivity analysis across epochs <em>E</em> &isin; {16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 1024} reveals that even at <em>E</em>=16, lag penalty exceeds 113&nbsp;ns, proving that rapid bursts require inline, per-acquisition responsiveness.</p>
        <p><strong>Cost-Benefit vs. Adaptive Mutex Tuning:</strong> Compared to simple adaptive mutex spinning (e.g., Linux's <code>osq_lock</code> heuristic, which merely tunes spin duration before sleeping), CLTML enables multi-primitive structural adaptation (e.g., switching between standard <code>qspinlock</code> and NUMA-aware cohorting) under cross-socket contention, while bounding decision latency to &lt;4&nbsp;ns with zero runtime FPU overhead.</p>

        <h3 class="subsec-heading">C. Scope and Delimitation</h3>
        <p>This work is structured as a deployment-aware constraint review combined with an empirical systems feasibility study. To maintain methodological rigor, we explicitly bound our claims across three tiers of evidence: (1) <em>Implemented &amp; Directly Measured:</em> Offline GBDT training, 12-bit branchless LUT compilation, C11 userspace microbenchmarks, 4-lock testbed across asymmetric cores on an [[HOST_CPU_CLEAN]], and an out-of-tree Linux 7.3.0 kernel module validating Tier 1 dynamic mutex spin-ceiling adaptation with zero sleep-in-atomic violations; (2) <em>Modeled NUMA Projection:</em> 64-core cross-socket latency scaling modeled via David et al. interconnect transfer cost equations; (3) <em>Proposed Upstream Architecture:</em> Tier 2 runtime lock primitive switching via RCU quiescent-state pointer swap, subject to LKMM formal constraints. Mainline kernel merge is not claimed.</p>
