        <h3 class="subsec-heading" style="margin-top: 0;">D. Literature Identification Methodology</h3>
        <p>This section reports a structured literature identification process informed by PRISMA reporting principles [4]; it is not claimed to be a fully compliant systematic literature review (SLR) protocol. We queried IEEE Xplore, ACM Digital Library, USENIX Proceedings, arXiv, and Semantic Scholar using three Boolean strings targeting lock contention, learned OS policies, and decision tree lookup tables. Initial database queries returned 243 candidate records: IEEE Xplore (<em>n</em>=78), ACM DL (<em>n</em>=84), USENIX (<em>n</em>=37), arXiv (<em>n</em>=26), and Semantic Scholar (<em>n</em>=18). Automated title and DOI deduplication removed 63 duplicate entries, leaving 180 unique candidate records. Both authors conducted independent screening against explicit criteria: (IC1) peer-reviewed systems venue with explicit synchronization evaluation; (IC2) in-kernel ML framework reporting inference latency; (IC3) tree compilation system relevant to LUT design. Dual-reviewer title/abstract screening on the 180 records yielded 173 concordant decisions (43 include, 130 exclude) and 7 disagreements (<em>P</em><sub>o</sub> = 96.11%, <em>P</em><sub>e</sub> = 61.68%, Cohen's &kappa; = 0.8985), establishing high inter-rater concordance.</p>
        <p>Full-text screening was performed on the remaining 40 candidate records, preserved with reviewer decisions in <code>data/screening_prisma_data.csv</code>. Dual-reviewer full-text screening yielded 37 concordant decisions (35 include, 2 exclude; <em>P</em><sub>o</sub> = 92.50%) and 3 borderline records (ID 36, 37, 39) resolved via joint consensus to exclude. The resulting corpus comprises 30 primary empirical systems studies from peer-reviewed venues meeting all IC1&ndash;IC5 criteria, complemented by 5 foundational reference texts, specifications, and hardware security advisories ([3], [6], [21], [33], [34], [35]) included during qualitative synthesis, and 1 benchmark standard ([36]), totaling 36 cited works. For each included study, five standard data fields were extracted: (1) target primitive & topology, (2) decision timing & scope, (3) decision latency budget vs. reported latency, (4) kernel safety constraints (zero FPU, bounded stack, IRQ safety), and (5) evaluation substrate.</p>

