        <h2 class="sec-heading">II. Survey of Multicore Linux Synchronization: Lineage, Capabilities, and Fallbacks</h2>

        <h3 class="subsec-heading">A. qspinlock Lineage and MCS Mechanics</h3>
        <p>Linux synchronization spans four primary families: spinlocks, sleeping mutexes, reader-writer locks, and RCU [3, 4]. Simple test-and-set and ticket spinlocks suffer severe coherence degradation beyond 8 to 16 threads on multi-socket machines because every contending core spins on a single shared cache line [1, 10].</p>

        <p>To eliminate cache-line bouncing without expanding <code>struct spinlock</code> beyond 32 bits (4 bytes), Linux 4.2 replaced ticket locks with <code>qspinlock</code> [6]. Designed by Long [6] building on the foundational queue-lock principles of Mellor-Crummey and Scott [28] and Herlihy et al. [33], <code>qspinlock</code> uses a 1-byte atomic lock word for uncontended fast paths; when two or more threads contend, waiters enqueue per-CPU MCS queue nodes and spin locally on private cache lines, achieving O(1) coherence bus traffic [4, 6]. While non-blocking and lock-free synchronization (Fraser and Harris [29]) eliminates lock words for specialized algorithms, general Linux kernel critical sections require mutual exclusion. However, standard <code>qspinlock</code> grants handoffs in strict FIFO order regardless of NUMA topology, triggering expensive cross-socket cache-line migrations under sustained multi-socket contention.</p>

        <h3 class="subsec-heading">B. NUMA-Aware Hierarchical Cohorting</h3>
        <p>To mitigate cross-socket coherence bouncing, Dice et al. [5] introduced lock cohorting, grouping threads by NUMA socket into local cohort queues. A socket-local lock passes ownership consecutively among local threads before transferring the global lock to a remote socket, yielding 2.1× to 3.2× throughput gains on 4-socket and 8-socket NUMA servers. Dice and Kogan [8] extended this with Compact NUMA-Aware (CNA) locks, which dynamically shuffle the MCS queue to favor local threads within a bounded cohort threshold (e.g., 256 consecutive local acquisitions). Kashyap et al. [9] developed ShflLock, which shuffles waiting nodes to balance locality and fairness. However, cohorting exhibits an inherent starvation failure mode: under saturated local load, remote threads can experience tail waiting times exceeding 500&nbsp;µs [2, 5].</p>

        <h3 class="subsec-heading">C. Read-Dominant Optimizations and RCU Grace Periods</h3>
        <p>For read-intensive kernel data structures (such as the directory cache and routing tables), Read-Copy-Update (RCU) [3, 7] eliminates synchronization overhead for readers by permitting concurrent read traversal without locks or atomic instructions. Updaters allocate a new copy of the modified data structure, atomically swap the pointer, and defer reclamation until all pre-existing readers complete an execution quiescent state (grace period). However, RCU is an architectural data-structure design choice, not a drop-in replacement for mutual exclusion: updaters still require mutual exclusion (e.g., spinlocks), and grace periods incur millisecond-scale latency and memory pressure when write ratios rise above 15% to 20% [3, 7]. Similarly, BRAVO (Biased Locking for Reader-Writer Locks) [32] accelerates rwlocks by maintaining per-CPU read flags, but degrades under frequent writer preemption.</p>

        <h3 class="subsec-heading">D. Hardware Speculative Lock Elision and TSX Deprecation</h3>
        <p>Hardware Transactional Memory (HTM), pioneered by Rajwar and Goodman [10] through Speculative Lock Elision (SLE) and commercialized in Intel TSX [11], executes critical sections speculatively without modifying lock state. In low-conflict workloads, TSX improves throughput by 18% to 35% [11]. However, TSX suffers severe performance collapse during transactional aborts, which force execution back to a fallback spinlock. Furthermore, the discovery of transient execution vulnerabilities—specifically Microarchitectural Data Sampling (MDS) and TSX Asynchronous Abort (TAA) [34, 35]—forced Intel and Linux kernel developers to disable TSX by default or restrict it via microcode updates on affected server and client processors, removing hardware speculation as a viable general-purpose kernel synchronization solution.</p>
