    <div class="cols">
        <h3 class="subsec-heading" style="margin-top: 0;">E. Dynamic Heuristics and Competitive Spinning</h3>
        <p>Because static primitives cannot adapt to shifting workloads, hybrid adaptive locks attempt to dynamically blend fast-path spinning with sleep waiting. In classic competitive analysis, Karlin et al. [13] proved that a 2-competitive spin-then-sleep lock spins for a duration equal to the context-switch cost before sleeping, bounding worst-case latency to twice the optimal offline schedule. Lim and Agarwal [14] introduced reactive synchronization, where locks adjust spinning thresholds based on past waiting times. The Linux kernel incorporates optimistic spinning in <code>kernel/locking/mutex.c</code>: a contending thread spins on an optimistic spinning queue (<code>osq_lock</code>) only if the lock owner is actively running on another CPU, aborting spin and sleeping immediately if the owner yields or reschedules [4]. SmartLocks (Eastep et al. [12]) dynamically adjusted lock acquisition scheduling in userspace via reinforcement learning. However, kernel heuristic tuners rely on coarse, hand-tuned thresholds that suffer 50 to 200&nbsp;ms adaptation lag during bursty transactional phase shifts.</p>

        <h3 class="subsec-heading">F. Taxonomy of Kernel Synchronization Adaptation</h3>
        <p class="no-indent">Based on our systematic review, we organize adaptive synchronization literature into a three-class taxonomy:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Class 1 (Contention Profiling &amp; Static Heuristic Selection):</strong> Profiling frameworks (e.g., Linux <code>lockstat</code>, <code>perf lock</code>, LockMeter, and studies by Boyd-Wickizer et al. [16] and Min et al. [17]) aggregate lock hold times, wait durations, and contention frequencies offline or out-of-band. While essential for identifying bottleneck call sites, Class 1 systems do not adapt primitive selection dynamically at runtime.</li>
            <li><strong>Class 2 (Online Reinforcement Learning &amp; Heuristic Tuning):</strong> Systems such as SmartLocks [12], Malthusian locks [22], generalized reinforcement learning frameworks (e.g., Mnih et al. [21]), and Linux mutex optimistic spinning adapt spin thresholds or acquisition schedules dynamically. However, Class 2 approaches either operate in userspace or restrict adaptation to single tunable scalar parameters (e.g., spin duration), failing to reconfigure the underlying synchronization primitive topology across NUMA boundaries.</li>
            <li><strong>Class 3 (Learned &amp; In-Kernel ML Frameworks):</strong> Recent efforts introduce machine learning models directly into OS kernels. LinnOS (Hao et al. [20], OSDI '20) embeds a lightweight neural network into the Linux block layer to predict SSD read latency, achieving microsecond-scale I/O admission control. KML (Agadakos et al. [31], HotStorage '20) embeds ML inference inside the Linux kernel for storage prefetching, managing floating-point state via <code>kernel_fpu_begin()</code>. SynCord (Ponnapalli et al. [30], OSDI '22) abstracts kernel synchronization policies, enabling user-defined synchronization tuning via eBPF. LAKE (Fingler et al. [15], ASPLOS '23) provides an asynchronous accelerator offload framework for kernel ML.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>Crucially, as established in Section III, no existing Class 3 system achieves in-band decision latency under 100&nbsp;ns, leaving a critical research gap at the intersection of machine learning and low-level kernel mutual exclusion.</p>
    

        <h2 class="sec-heading">III. Related Work and the Runtime Inference Latency Wall</h2>

        <h3 class="subsec-heading">A. In-Kernel Machine Learning Frameworks</h3>
        <p>The success of machine learning across computer systems has driven significant interest in learned OS policies. As summarized in Table II, existing in-kernel ML frameworks target subsystems with microsecond-to-millisecond latency budgets. LinnOS [20] embeds a lightweight neural network in the Linux block layer to predict flash storage latency with a 4 to 6&nbsp;µs inference budget. KML [31] embeds multi-layer perceptrons into the Linux kernel for storage prefetching, managing floating-point context switches via <code>kernel_fpu_begin()</code> and <code>kernel_fpu_end()</code>. LAKE [15] introduces an asynchronous offloading engine routing inference to discrete accelerators via shared ring buffers, incurring 2 to 10&nbsp;µs transfer latency. In packet processing, the eXpress Data Path (XDP) [18] demonstrates that fast-path in-kernel hooks must complete within tens of nanoseconds. SynCord [30] abstracts kernel synchronization policies, enabling user-defined synchronization tuning via eBPF.</p>

        <h3 class="subsec-heading">B. Primary Research Gap: Runtime Inference Latency vs. 100 ns Fast-Path Budget (RQ2)</h3>
        <p>While a 4 to 10&nbsp;µs decision latency is well suited for block I/O or network packet scheduling, it fails completely inside synchronization primitives. The Linux kernel lock fast path—the window between the atomic CAS instruction and critical section entry—is bounded by a strict budget of 50 to 100&nbsp;ns. In our native C11 benchmark (<code>cltml_benchmark.c</code>), evaluating an offline-trained [[NUM_TREES]]-tree GBDT ensemble ([[NUM_NODES]] nodes) requires [[LAT_GBDT]]&nbsp;ns ([[CYC_GBDT]] CPU cycles), exceeding the 100&nbsp;ns fast-path budget by 3.5×. Even an integer-quantized, branchless GBDT requires [[LAT_INT_GBDT]]&nbsp;ns ([[CYC_INT_GBDT]] cycles), consuming almost the entire 100&nbsp;ns budget on tree evaluation alone.</p>

        <p>Standard software mitigations cannot resolve this dilemma: (1) <em>Asynchronous background prediction</em> runs inference on a helper thread every 10 to 50&nbsp;ms, but our empirical trace simulations reveal severe staleness during bursty transactional workloads, causing prediction accuracy to drop from 95.23% to 61.4% over moderate 100-to-200-acquisition windows, and collapsing to 21.95% over coarse 1,024-acquisition epochs (Table IV); (2) <em>Aggressive tree pruning</em> down to 1–2 shallow decision stumps degrades accuracy to that of a static heuristic; and (3) <em>Hardware accelerator offload</em> (LAKE [15]) incurs microsecond PCIe roundtrip latency.</p>

        <h3 class="subsec-heading">C. Secondary Research Gaps</h3>
        <p class="no-indent">Four additional architectural gaps must be addressed for real kernel deployment:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Gap 2 (Workload-Shift Detection &amp; Table Refresh):</strong> Static compiled tables degrade if workload characteristics drift outside the training distribution. A background kernel worker thread (<code>kworker</code>) must monitor tail latency drift and atomically update the table pointer via RCU (<code>rcu_assign_pointer</code>).</li>
            <li><strong>Gap 3 (Safe Constrained Adaptation):</strong> Policy exploration must be restricted to verified Pareto-optimal primitives to eliminate tail latency divergence.</li>
            <li><strong>Gap 4 (Architecture-Portable Feature Abstraction):</strong> Raw hardware counter event encodings vary widely across Intel, AMD, and ARM processors [36]. Normalizing counters into scale-invariant ratios enables portable policy tables.</li>
            <li><strong>Gap 5 (Formal Verification of Hybrid Control):</strong> While the Linux Kernel Memory Model (LKMM) [26] and Coq proofs [27] verify individual primitives, formal verification must guarantee that runtime policy transitions preserve mutual exclusion and deadlock freedom.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="subsec-heading">D. Related Work: Compiled Tree Ensembles</h3>
        <p>Several frameworks compile decision trees into native code or lookup structures. QuickScorer (Lucchese et al. [23]) evaluates GBDT scoring forests via bit-parallel traversal over sorted threshold vectors. TreeLUT (Khataei and Bazargan [24]) maps decision trees into FPGA lookup tables. Treelite (Cho and Chen [25]) compiles decision trees into nested C conditionals. While Treelite avoids interpreter overhead, evaluating branching conditionals across an ensemble of 10 trees of depth 4 incurs up to 40 branches; on modern superscalar CPUs, unpredictable branches trigger 12–18 cycle pipeline flushes, accumulating 80–150&nbsp;ns in branch misprediction penalties. In contrast, CLTML precomputes all tree ensemble outputs across the 12-bit quantized space into a flat 4&nbsp;KB array, executing via branchless arithmetic (<code>index = (b0 &lt;&lt; 8) | (b1 &lt;&lt; 4) | b2; policy = lut[index];</code>) in 10.1 cycles (3.76&nbsp;ns) with zero branch mispredictions, zero floating-point operations, and an L1-resident footprint. To our knowledge, within the specific intersection of kernel lock fast-path selection, zero-FPU fixed-point inference, L1-cache-bounded footprint, and context-immutability enforcement, CLTML is the first compile-time GBDT-to-LUT compiler designed for this constrained domain. This claim is provisional and conditioned on the absence of prior work satisfying constraints (a)–(e) simultaneously: (a) zero FPU instructions, (b) deterministic O(1) execution with a branchless index, (c) 4&nbsp;KB L1-resident footprint, (d) &lt;64&nbsp;B stack overhead, and (e) context-immutability at quiescent epochs. QuickScorer, TreeLUT, and Treelite satisfy none of (a)–(e) simultaneously.</p>
    
