        <h2 class="sec-heading" style="margin-top: 0;">IV. The CLTML Architecture &amp; Formal Synchronization Contract</h2>

        <h3 class="subsec-heading">A. When Latency Matters: Per-Acquisition vs. Per-Epoch Amortization</h3>
        <p>An essential architectural question is whether lock selection must execute on every acquisition or whether decisions can be amortized across epochs. If a lock selector executes once per epoch (e.g., every 1,024 acquisitions or every 10&nbsp;ms), the runtime cost of even a 1,000&nbsp;ns model is amortized to less than 1&nbsp;ns per acquisition. Why, then, is a compile-time lookup table necessary? CLTML is required not merely for raw speed, but because it satisfies five mandatory kernel architectural imperatives:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Zero Floating-Point Unit (FPU) Usage:</strong> Standard ML libraries require floating-point arithmetic. In the Linux kernel, FPU instructions cannot be used without wrapping calls in <code>kernel_fpu_begin()</code> and <code>kernel_fpu_end()</code>, which save and restore 512 bytes of AVX/SSE register state, costing 80 to 150 cycles and strictly prohibited in interrupt or atomic context. CLTML executes entirely in fixed-point integer math.</li>
            <li><strong>Deterministic O(1) Bounded Execution:</strong> Branching tree traversals trigger branch mispredictions and tail latency spikes. CLTML evaluates via branchless index math and a single direct array lookup in O(1) time.</li>
            <li><strong>L1 Cache-Resident Footprint:</strong> A 4,096-byte lookup table fits entirely within a single 4&nbsp;KB memory page and consumes only 64 cache lines (12.5% of a 32&nbsp;KB L1 data cache), ensuring it remains warm without displacing application working sets.</li>
            <li><strong>Strict Kernel Stack Bounds:</strong> Linux kernel thread stacks are strictly limited to 8&nbsp;KB or 16&nbsp;KB. Complex model inference risks stack overflow; CLTML requires less than 64 bytes of stack space.</li>
            <li><strong>Exhaustive Formal Verifiability:</strong> Because the state space is exactly 2<sup>12</sup> = 4,096 entries, every possible input vector can be exhaustively audited and validated against safety predicates at compile time prior to kernel insertion.</li>
        </ol>

        <h3 class="subsec-heading">B. Offline Quantization and 12-Bit Table Compilation (RQ3)</h3>
        <p>CLTML compiles an offline-trained GBDT into a static, read-only 4,096-byte C array (<code>static const uint8_t cltml_lookup_table[4096]</code>). Training proceeds in two stages:</p>

        <p><strong>Stage 1 (Offline GBDT Training &amp; Gain Ranking):</strong> An offline Second-Order Gradient-Boosted Decision Tree ([[NUM_TREES]] trees, depth 4, [[NUM_NODES]] nodes) is trained on 8 hardware counter features: <em>F</em><sub>0</sub> (<code>llc_miss_rate</code>), <em>F</em><sub>1</sub> (<code>cross_numa_traffic</code>), <em>F</em><sub>2</sub> (<code>lock_queue_depth</code>), <em>F</em><sub>3</sub> (<code>read_write_ratio</code>), <em>F</em><sub>4</sub> (<code>cas_retry_rate</code>), <em>F</em><sub>5</sub> (<code>hold_time_cv</code>), <em>F</em><sub>6</sub> (<code>l3_hit_latency_ns</code>), and <em>F</em><sub>7</sub> (<code>cpu_runq_len</code>), labeled with the lowest-latency Oracle primitive:</p>

        <div class="equation-container">
            <div class="eq-math"><em>y</em><sup>*</sup> = <span class="op">arg&thinsp;min</span><sub><em>p</em> &isin; <span class="op">&#119979;</span></sub> <em>C</em><sub><em>p</em></sub>(<strong>x</strong>)</div>
            <div class="eq-num">(1)</div>
        </div>

        <p class="no-indent">where 0 = <code>qspinlock</code> (uncontended flat topology), 1 = <code>CNA / Cohort Lock</code> (NUMA-aware hierarchical), 2 = <code>Adaptive Mutex Low-Spin</code> (sleep-waiting under heavy queue depth), and 3 = <code>Adaptive Mutex High-Spin</code> (optimistic spinning under low contention). RCU is formalized as an architectural read-mostly synchronization design, not an inline drop-in mutex replacement. Split-gain feature importance analysis confirms that the top 4 features capture 98.94% of total split gain: <em>F</em><sub>1</sub> (<code>cross_numa_traffic</code>, 46.26%), <em>F</em><sub>2</sub> (<code>lock_queue_depth</code>, 27.95%), <em>F</em><sub>4</sub> (<code>cas_retry_rate</code>, 24.13%), and <em>F</em><sub>0</sub> (<code>llc_miss_rate</code>, 0.60%). The remaining features contribute minimally: <em>F</em><sub>3</sub> (0.36%), <em>F</em><sub>7</sub> (0.25%), <em>F</em><sub>6</sub> (0.25%), and <em>F</em><sub>5</sub> (0.20%), summing to exactly 100.00%.</p>

        <p><strong>Stage 2 (Exact 12-Bit Quantization):</strong> To construct an exact 4,096-byte table (2<sup>12</sup> entries, 1 byte per entry), we formulate and evaluate two quantization configurations:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Design A (4 Features &times; 3 Bits = 12 Bits &rarr; 8<sup>4</sup> = 4,096 B):</strong> Allocates 3 bits (8 equal-frequency bins, 7 thresholds) to each of the top 4 features. Evaluates branchlessly via <code>idx = (b0 &lt;&lt; 9) | (b1 &lt;&lt; 6) | (b2 &lt;&lt; 3) | b3</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Design B (3 Features &times; 4 Bits = 12 Bits &rarr; 16<sup>3</sup> = 4,096 B):</strong> Allocates 4 bits (16 bins, 15 thresholds) to each of the top 3 features (<em>F</em><sub>3</sub>, <em>F</em><sub>1</sub>, <em>F</em><sub>2</sub>).</li>
        </ul>
        <p><em>Sensitivity Analysis (Design A vs. Design B):</em> Although Design B quantizes one fewer feature, 4-bit binning requires searching across 15 thresholds per feature (versus 7 for Design A). In scalar arithmetic, this incurs $3 \times 15 = 45$ comparisons, compared to $4 \times 7 = 28$ comparisons in Design A, increasing quantization latency to 5.82&nbsp;ns (versus 3.76&nbsp;ns). Storing 1 byte per entry (`uint8_t table[4096]`) allows single-instruction memory loads (`movzbl`). Packing four 2-bit entries per byte would compress the table to 1,024&nbsp;bytes, but extracting entries would require multi-cycle bit-shifting and masking (`shr`, `and`), adding 2&ndash;3 cycles on the critical path. Because modern L1D caches provide 32&ndash;48&nbsp;KB, byte alignment is the optimal low-latency choice.</p>

        
