    <div class="cols">
        <h3 class="subsec-heading">C. Formal Synchronization Contract &amp; Cardinal Invariant 1</h3>
        <p>A fatal pitfall in dynamic kernel locking is violating kernel execution context rules. A sleeping mutex cannot replace a spinlock in atomic or interrupt context: invoking <code>schedule()</code> or acquiring a sleeping lock in hardirq, softirq, or RCU read-side critical sections triggers a fatal kernel panic (<code>BUG: scheduling while atomic</code>). To ensure sound theoretical grounding, CLTML enforces Cardinal Invariant 1 (Context Immutability):</p>

        <div class="equation-container">
            <div class="eq-math" style="font-size: 6.6pt; white-space: nowrap;">&forall;<em>t</em>, &nbsp; <span class="op">Context</span>(<em>t</em>) &isin; {<span class="op">HARDIRQ, SOFTIRQ, RCU_READ</span>} &rArr; &not;<span class="op">CanSleep</span>(<span class="op">Primitive</span>(<em>t</em>))</div>
            <div class="eq-num">(4)</div>
        </div>

        <p><strong>Formal Context Definitions &amp; Panic Mechanics:</strong> (1) <em>HARDIRQ:</em> Asynchronous top-half hardware interrupt handlers with local interrupts disabled (<code>in_hardirq() != 0</code>); sleeping halts the interrupt pipeline and freezes the CPU. (2) <em>SOFTIRQ:</em> Bottom-half handlers (timers, network NAPI) running with preemption disabled (<code>preempt_count() &gt; 0</code>); invoking <code>schedule()</code> is strictly illegal. (3) <em>RCU_READ:</em> Critical sections enclosed by <code>rcu_read_lock()</code> / <code>rcu_read_unlock()</code>; sleeping delays grace periods indefinitely, causing RCU stall warnings. If a sleeping lock (<code>mutex_lock</code>) is invoked when <code>in_atomic() != 0</code>, <code>kernel/sched/core.c</code> executes <code>schedule_debug()</code>, emitting <code>BUG: scheduling while atomic</code> and triggering an unrecoverable kernel panic. Non-sleepable contexts are restricted to non-sleeping spinlocks (<code>raw_spinlock_t</code>, <code>qspinlock</code>, <code>CNA</code>); sleeping mutexes are permitted solely in process context.</p>

        <p>To enforce Invariant (4), CLTML implements a <strong>Two-Tier Architecture</strong>:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Tier 1 (In-Band Adaptive Spin-Ceiling Tuning):</strong> Within sleepable primitives (such as <code>struct mutex</code>), waiters optimistically spin using <code>osq_lock</code>. Tier 1 dynamically tunes the spin ceiling (<em>S</em><sub>ceiling</sub> ∈ [50, 2000] iterations) using the 4&nbsp;KB LUT. Because the primitive remains a sleepable mutex throughout, sleep/wake semantics are strictly preserved with zero risk of panic.</li>
            <li><strong>Tier 2 (Task-Context Macro-Selection):</strong> Runtime primitive switching is strictly confined to compatible primitives:
                <ul>
                    <li><em>Atomic/IRQ Context:</em> Swapping is restricted between <code>qspinlock</code> and NUMA-aware <code>CNA</code> spinlocks.</li>
                    <li><em>Sleepable Context:</em> Swapping is restricted to tuning adaptive mutexes or switching between sleep-capable locks.</li>
                    <li><em>RCU Optimization:</em> Treated as an architectural subsystem design choice; writers continue to use mutual exclusion.</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p><em>Scope note & Tier 2 Safe Handover Protocol:</em> Tier 2 primitive switching is an architectural proposal evaluated in userspace multi-lock benchmarks. To safely switch primitives at runtime without data corruption, a lock incorporates a <code>LOCK_DRAINING</code> state. When a primitive switch is requested, incoming contenders spin on an outer gate while active queue waiters drain under the old primitive. Once <code>active_waiters == 0</code> (quiescent point), the lock word is atomically reinitialized to the target primitive and the gate is opened. If an interrupt handler sleeps on a mutex held by the interrupted thread, the CPU cannot switch tasks, triggering a fatal deadlock or panic (<code>BUG: scheduling while atomic</code>). The out-of-tree kernel module (<code>cltml_module.ko</code>) validates Tier 1 spin-ceiling tuning on the mutex slow path; Tier 2 primitive switching is evaluated in userspace.</p>

        <h3 class="subsec-heading">D. Live-Kernel Feasibility and Mutex Fast-Path Integration</h3>
        <p>To verify runtime feasibility within the Linux locking subsystem, we trace the integration of CLTML into <code>kernel/locking/mutex.c</code>. In mainline Linux, <code>__mutex_lock_common()</code> attempts an optimistic spin via <code>mutex_optimistic_spin()</code> prior to parking contending tasks on the wait queue. Waiters poll the lock owner and contend on the Optimistic Spinning Queue (<code>struct optimistic_spin_queue osq</code>). CLTML interfaces at this interception point: before acquiring <code>osq_lock()</code>, the contending task evaluates <code>cltml_kernel_select_4x3()</code> using thread-local counters. The 12-bit LUT output dynamically scales the spin threshold (<em>S</em><sub>ceiling</sub>), balancing CPU spinning against descheduling. Our prototype is implemented as an out-of-tree Linux kernel module (<code>cltml_module.ko</code>) providing an adaptive lock abstraction (<code>cltml_adaptive_lock_t</code>) that models this exact integration point. Because CLTML operates entirely via fixed-point integer arithmetic and consumes zero heap allocations or FPU state, it executes with zero risk of stack overflow (GCC <code>-fstack-usage</code> confirms 8 to 32 bytes) or scheduling panics.</p>
    

        <h3 class="subsec-heading" style="margin-top: 0;">E. Freestanding Kernel C LUT Implementation &amp; Branchless Disassembly</h3>
        <p>Because floating-point math is prohibited in fast-path kernel code, all quantization cutpoints are exported in <code>cltml_kernel_lut.h</code> as 32-bit fixed-point integers (<code>int32_t</code>, scaled by 100). Listing 1 shows the verified C implementation. Inspection of the compiled x86_64 disassembly (<code>objdump -d</code>) confirms that the quantization compiles branchlessly without a single conditional branch instruction (<code>jmp</code>), utilizing SIMD parallel comparisons (<code>pcmpgtd</code>) or scalar <code>cmp</code>, <code>setg</code>, and <code>add</code> sequences. Crucially, array indexing is strictly independent of comparison outcomes, eliminating data-dependent memory loads and branch mispredictions entirely.</p>

        <div class="code-container">
            <div class="code-caption">Listing 1: Freestanding Kernel C Header Implementation (<code>cltml_kernel_lut.h</code>).</div>
<pre class="code-block">
#ifndef _CLTML_KERNEL_LUT_H
#define _CLTML_KERNEL_LUT_H
#include &lt;stdint.h&gt;
#define CLTML_TABLE_SIZE 4096

/* Branchless 3-bit quantizer (zero data-dependent memory loads) */
static inline uint32_t cltml_quantize_3bit(int32_t v, const int32_t t[7]) {
    return (uint32_t)(v &gt; t[0]) + (uint32_t)(v &gt; t[1]) +
           (uint32_t)(v &gt; t[2]) + (uint32_t)(v &gt; t[3]) +
           (uint32_t)(v &gt; t[4]) + (uint32_t)(v &gt; t[5]) +
           (uint32_t)(v &gt; t[6]);
}

static inline uint8_t cltml_kernel_select_4x3(const int32_t f[8], int en) {
    uint32_t b0 = cltml_quantize_3bit(f[1], cltml_th_numa);   /* F1 */
    uint32_t b1 = cltml_quantize_3bit(f[2], cltml_th_qdepth); /* F2 */
    uint32_t b2 = cltml_quantize_3bit(f[4], cltml_th_cas);    /* F4 */
    uint32_t b3 = cltml_quantize_3bit(f[0], cltml_th_llc);    /* F0 */
    uint32_t idx = ((b0 &lt;&lt; 9) | (b1 &lt;&lt; 6) | (b2 &lt;&lt; 3) | b3) &amp; 0x0FFFu;
    uint8_t sel = cltml_kernel_lut_4x3[idx];
    return (uint8_t)(en ? sel : 0); /* Compiled to cmovz: zero jmp */
}
#endif
</pre>
        </div>

        <h3 class="subsec-heading">F. Microarchitectural Comparison with Treelite and Shallow Decision Trees</h3>
        <p>Prior model compilation frameworks such as Treelite [25] compile tree ensembles into nested C <code>if-else</code> conditionals. However, an ensemble of 10 depth-4 trees introduces up to 40 conditional branches. Under unpredictable hardware telemetry, conditional branches trigger 12&ndash;18 cycle pipeline flushes. Even a single depth-3 decision tree (7 nodes, 112&nbsp;B code) requires 12.40&nbsp;ns (33.3 cycles) and suffers branch mispredictions under phase transitions (tail p99 latency spikes to 31.8&nbsp;ns). In contrast, CLTML compiles the ensemble into a 4&nbsp;KB table indexed branchlessly using bitwise shifts and additions, executing in 3.76&nbsp;ns (10.1 cycles, 3.3&times; faster than the depth-3 tree) with deterministic tail latency (p99 = 4.26&nbsp;ns) and zero branch mispredictions. Furthermore, depth-3 trees are capacity-limited to 8 leaves; scaling to complex 4-feature interactions requires deeper ensembles, which increase tree evaluation time linearly to 90&ndash;335&nbsp;ns, whereas LUT lookup latency remains strictly <em>O(1)</em> constant time.</p>

        
