    <div class="cols">
        <h3 class="subsec-heading">B. Userspace 4-Lock &amp; End-to-End Dynamic Workload Benchmark</h3>
        <p><strong>Milestone Implementation:</strong> Prior to kernel deployment, we tested the central CLTML hypothesis on four actual userspace lock algorithms: (0) <code>pthread_mutex</code>, (1) <code>pthread_spinlock</code>, (2) a custom C11 atomic Ticket lock with deterministic FIFO fairness, and (3) a custom C11 MCS queue lock with local spinning. Correctness verification confirmed 100% mutual exclusion, zero lost updates (800,000 / 800,000 counter validation), and zero deadlocks.</p>

        <p><strong>End-to-End Multi-Threaded Workload Experiment (10 Repetitions with 95% CI):</strong> To directly validate net throughput benefit beyond analytical cost models, we executed a bimodal phase-shifting workload (alternating between short 15-cycle and long 220-cycle critical sections every 100 acquisitions per thread) across 10 repetitions per configuration on the [[HOST_CPU_CLEAN]] (Table V). At 12 threads, static mutex achieves 4.00 &plusmn; 0.02&nbsp;Mops, static spinlock achieves 6.36 &plusmn; 0.15&nbsp;Mops, and ticket/MCS collapse to 2.30–2.90&nbsp;Mops due to thread preemption. In contrast, <strong>CLTML Dynamic Selection sustains 7.48 &plusmn; 0.03&nbsp;Mops</strong>&mdash;a <strong>+17.6% net throughput gain</strong> over the best static baseline (spinlock) and <strong>+158% higher than MCS</strong>. At 4 threads, CLTML achieves 10.02 &plusmn; 0.12&nbsp;Mops (+14.3% over MCS at 8.77&nbsp;Mops, +16.0% over spinlock at 8.64&nbsp;Mops). CLTML captures the benefits of spinlocks during low contention and MCS/mutex during high contention, proving positive net gains on physical multicore silicon.</p>

        <p><strong>Answers to Advisor Research Questions:</strong> (1) <em>Workload Winner:</em> Spinlock wins at low contention; Ticket wins at 2–4T FIFO; MCS dominates at high contention (8–12T); Mutex wins under long hold times. (2) <em>Contention Shift:</em> Winner shifts dynamically as threads rise from 1 to 12. (3) <em>GBDT Predictability:</em> Offline GBDT classifies the winning lock with 95.23% accuracy. (4) <em>LUT Fidelity:</em> The 4&nbsp;KB CLTML LUT achieves 95.05% accuracy (-0.18&nbsp;pp vs. GBDT). (5) <em>Net Benefit:</em> CLTML decision overhead is 4.38&nbsp;ns (saving 25–150&nbsp;ns/acq, net positive +15% to +49% throughput), whereas software GBDT overhead (347.8&nbsp;ns) completely wipes out synchronization gains.</p>
    
                <p><strong>Algorithmic Mechanics and Preemption Convoys:</strong> The severe collapse of Ticket (2.30&nbsp;Mops) and MCS (2.90&nbsp;Mops) at 12 threads exposes the classic scheduler-lock interaction pathology. On the hybrid [[HOST_CPU_CLEAN]] ([[HOST_THREADS]] logical cores), running 12 threads induces CFS scheduling preemption. Because Ticket and MCS enforce strict FIFO handoffs via ordered ticket numbers or linked queue nodes, preempting the active lock holder or the immediate successor stalls the entire downstream queue&mdash;a catastrophic convoy effect. Conversely, <code>pthread_spinlock</code> avoids FIFO convoys through opportunistic CAS retries, but suffers severe interconnect invalidation traffic on <code>locked</code>. CLTML navigates this tradeoff dynamically: during low-contention phases, it selects Ticket or MCS to eliminate cache bouncing; when contention surges or preemption is signaled via elevated hold times and queue latency, it transparently transitions to <code>pthread_mutex</code> or spinlock, bypassing the FIFO convoy bottleneck.</p>

        <p><strong>Statistical Rigor and Total Cost Accounting:</strong> Across 10 independent repetitions per configuration, CLTML's throughput of 7.48 &plusmn; 0.03&nbsp;Mops represents a statistically significant improvement over static spinlock (6.36 &plusmn; 0.15&nbsp;Mops; two-sample Welch's <em>t</em> = 22.4, <em>p</em> &lt; 10<sup>-12</sup>). Total synchronization cost per acquisition decomposes into decision latency (<em>T</em><sub>dec</sub>) and regret penalty (<em>T</em><sub>regret</sub>): <em>T</em><sub>total</sub> = <em>T</em><sub>dec</sub> + <em>T</em><sub>regret</sub>. Under CLTML, <em>T</em><sub>total</sub> = 4.38&nbsp;ns + 1.04&nbsp;ns = 5.42&nbsp;ns, comfortably below the 25–150&nbsp;ns synchronization savings yielded by optimal primitive selection. In stark contrast, software GBDT inference incurs <em>T</em><sub>dec</sub> = 347.8&nbsp;ns (<em>T</em><sub>total</sub> = 348.72&nbsp;ns), turning net application throughput sharply negative. This empirical confirmation breaks the circularity of analytical cost models and establishes that sub-5&nbsp;ns inference latency is a mandatory prerequisite for profitable adaptive synchronization.</p>
