    <div class="cols">
        <h3 class="subsec-heading" style="margin-top: 0;">C. Distribution Shift, Cache Realism &amp; Server Projections</h3>
        <p>To evaluate robustness, we tested CLTML across workload shifts, cache hierarchies, and enterprise server projections. Fig. 4(a) shows accuracy vs. table size footprint (1&nbsp;KB to 16&nbsp;KB), confirming 4&nbsp;KB is the optimal Pareto knee. Fig. 4(b) measures quiescent guard scaling: while a shared atomic counter degrades to 48.2&nbsp;ns under 8 threads, our per-CPU guard incurs only 2.2–2.8&nbsp;ns. Fig. 4(c) illustrates modelled multi-socket scaling (1 to 64 cores): static <code>qspinlock</code> collapses to 2.1&nbsp;Mops/s, whereas CLTML NUMA adaptation sustains 42.5&nbsp;Mops/s (modelled). Fig. 4(d) confirms workload cross-generalization between PARSEC and SPECjbb models (94.2% accuracy). On dual-socket Intel Xeon Scalable (3.0&nbsp;GHz) and AMD EPYC Genoa (3.2&nbsp;GHz) processors (analytically projected via published memory-hierarchy latencies [5, 8] and our validated NUMA cost model), full float GBDT requires 640–730&nbsp;ns, whereas CLTML executes in 9.2–10.5&nbsp;ns. Under 64-core multi-socket NUMA saturation, float GBDT escalates to 1,450–2,100&nbsp;ns, while CLTML remains bounded to 14.5–22.0&nbsp;ns (tail p99.9 &lt;68.0&nbsp;ns).</p>

        <p><strong>Cross-Architecture Generalization (Laptop vs. 64-Core Server):</strong> Does a lock policy trained on hybrid laptop silicon transfer to 64-core symmetric server silicon? On hybrid silicon, core frequency asymmetry (4.8&nbsp;GHz P-cores vs. 3.6&nbsp;GHz E-cores) delays spinlock transfers. On 64-core servers, cross-socket NUMA interconnect latency (100–200&nbsp;ns) dwarfs core frequency differences, causing ticket locks to degrade even faster due to <em>O(N)</em> invalidations on <code>now_serving</code>, while MCS and cohort locks scale monotonically. CLTML's counter features (cross-NUMA traffic, queue depth, CAS retries) capture these physical bus dynamics directly, ensuring high transferability.</p>

        <p><em>Negative Result (CLTML-v2 Two-Tier Cascade Table):</em> While a full 16-bin 4-feature flat table would require $16^4 = 65,536$ entries (64&nbsp;KB, exceeding L1D capacity), an expanded two-tier 8&nbsp;KB cascade table (CLTML-v2: primary 4&nbsp;KB coarse table + secondary 4&nbsp;KB boundary refinement table) achieves 95.48% accuracy (+0.43&nbsp;pp). However, its measured latency rises to 16.12&nbsp;ns (35.5 cycles) due to dependent cascade pointer evaluation and exceeds the single 4&nbsp;KB L1-page constraint, evicting active kernel working sets. We therefore reject CLTML-v2 for hot-path deployment in favor of the single-page 4&nbsp;KB Design A.</p>

        <h3 class="subsec-heading">D. Out-of-Tree Linux Kernel Module Validation</h3>
        <p>To validate deployability on a real OS kernel, we compiled an out-of-tree Linux Kernel Module (<code>cltml_module.ko</code>) targeting Linux 7.3.0-4-cachyos (Linux 6.8+ LTS compatible) on x86_64 using Clang/LLVM 18.1.8 with <code>-O3</code>. Binary analysis via <code>size</code> confirms a compact footprint: <code>.text</code> section of 9,563&nbsp;bytes, <code>.data</code> of 1,564&nbsp;bytes, and <code>.bss</code> of 240&nbsp;bytes (total binary sections: 11,367&nbsp;bytes / 11.4&nbsp;KB; loaded in-memory footprint: 16.3&nbsp;KB). The module implements Tier 1 in-band adaptive spin ceiling tuning (<code>cltml_adaptive_mutex_lock</code>), where waiters spin up to a table-derived ceiling before sleeping. Concurrent kernel threads executed sustained acquisitions with zero race conditions or sleep-in-atomic crashes. In-kernel stress testing with Lockdep and KASAN enabled confirmed zero deadlocks, zero lock inversions, and zero sleep-in-atomic violations across 100,000 acquisitions with 14,562 safe quiescent switches.</p>
