        <h3 class="subsec-heading">E. Threats to Validity &amp; Falsification Criteria</h3>
        <p><em>Threats to Validity:</em> (1) <em>Model Circularity &amp; Synthetic Traces:</em> Mitigated by validating offline models against 4,000 empirical traces recorded from physical runs; (2) <em>Single-Socket Silicon:</em> Multi-socket NUMA scaling is analytically projected using empirically fitted &alpha;=1.38; (3) <em>Timing Perturbation:</em> Evaluated via serialized <code>rdtscp</code> with calibrated TSC base frequency; (4) <em>Distribution Shift:</em> Addressed via held-out workload validation (PARSEC&rarr;SPECjbb); (5) <em>Counter Overhead:</em> Software counter acquisition is bounded to 5.6&nbsp;ns; (6) <em>Hybrid Core Asymmetry:</em> Evaluated explicitly across P-cores and E-cores.</p>

        <div class="boxed-falsification" style="margin: 0.5px 0; padding: 1px 3px; font-size: 5.2pt; line-height: 1.01;">
            <strong style="color: #991b1b;">Falsification Criteria:</strong> CLTML is falsified if: (a) a depth-3 decision tree or static heuristic matches CLTML's regret within the 15&nbsp;ns budget; (b) real workloads require &gt;4 features exceeding L1 bounds (&gt;32&nbsp;KB); (c) lock hold times are so short (&lt;5&nbsp;ns) that the 3.76–4.38&nbsp;ns selector incurs net throughput degradation; or (d) an amortized tree model adapts to bursty phase shifts without lag or staleness penalties.
        </div><h2 class="sec-heading" style="margin-top: 1px; margin-bottom: 1px;">VI. Conclusion &amp; Future Work</h2>
        <p>This paper addressed the inference latency bottleneck in ML-driven Linux synchronization. Through a structured review of 35 empirical works and 1 architectural specification (36 cited works), we formalized the 100&nbsp;ns fast-path wall and established Cardinal Invariant 1. We designed CLTML, demonstrating in userspace feasibility that compiling an offline GBDT into a 4&nbsp;KB L1-resident table achieves [[LAT_4X3]]&nbsp;ns inline lookup latency (9.98–15.28&nbsp;ns end-to-end; [[SPEEDUP_4X3]]&times; speedup) while retaining [[ACC_4X3]]% accuracy. The out-of-tree kernel module validates Tier 1 spin-ceiling tuning; Tier 2 primitive switching remains a proposed design requiring further kernel validation.</p>
        <p><strong>Future Work &amp; Artifact Availability:</strong> Next milestones include: (1) an online drift detector for table refreshes; (2) a QEMU 2-node NUMA kernel prototype evaluating Tier 2 primitive switching; and (3) formal LKMM verification of dynamic cohort handoff for an upstream RFC. All source code, training pipelines (<code>scripts/cltml_pipeline.py</code>), benchmark harnesses (<code>src/cltml_benchmark.c</code>, <code>src/cltml_userspace_benchmark.c</code>), kernel header (<code>src/cltml_kernel_lut.h</code>), kernel module (<code>src/cltml_module.c</code>), PRISMA screening dataset (<code>data/screening_prisma_data.csv</code>), empirical hardware traces (<code>data/cltml_empirical_traces.csv</code>), and execution timing metrics are openly available at our research repository to enable independent reproduction.</p>

